(4. Heuristic Function) Tj
0 -12 Td
(The heuristic h\(n\) estimates how close a node n is to the goal. In this assignment's sample) Tj
0 -12 Td
(cases, lower heuristic values indicate locations that appear closer to the affected area.) Tj
0 -12 Td
(GBFS ignores accumulated path cost while choosing the next node; it expands the frontier) Tj
0 -12 Td
(node with the smallest h\(n\). This makes the search fast and goal-directed, but it does not) Tj
0 -12 Td
(guarantee the minimum-cost path for all weighted graphs.) Tj
0 -12 Td
() Tj
0 -12 Td
(5. Data Structures and Algorithm) Tj
0 -12 Td
(The graph is stored as an adjacency list: node -> list of \(neighbor, cost\). Heuristic values) Tj
0 -12 Td
(are stored in a dictionary for O\(1\) lookup. The frontier is a bounded min-priority queue) Tj
0 -12 Td
(implemented using heapq. The queue explicitly raises an error if insertion is attempted when) Tj
0 -12 Td
(full or deletion is attempted when empty, satisfying the data-structure error-handling) Tj
0 -12 Td
(requirement. A visited set prevents repeated expansion of the same node. Each priority queue) Tj
0 -12 Td
(entry stores heuristic value, insertion order, current node, path so far, and accumulated) Tj
0 -12 Td
(cost.) Tj
0 -12 Td
() Tj
0 -12 Td
(Algorithm steps:) Tj
0 -12 Td
(1. Read graph, heuristics, start node, and goal node from the input file.) Tj
0 -12 Td
(2. Insert the start node in the priority queue with priority h\(start\).) Tj
0 -12 Td
(3. Repeatedly remove the node with the lowest heuristic value.) Tj
0 -12 Td
(4. If the node is the goal, print expansion order, path, cost, measured time, and measured) Tj
0 -12 Td
(memory.) Tj
0 -12 Td
(5. Otherwise, insert all unvisited neighbors with priority h\(neighbor\).) Tj
0 -12 Td
(6. If the frontier becomes empty, report that no route exists.) Tj
0 -12 Td
() Tj
